---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title={"Maintenance | Roos Instruments"}
  description={"Cassini Support and Maintenance.  TIM Exchange Checklist and more."}
  active={["nav-support"]}>
    <!-- ==========================
    	BREADCRUMB - START
    =========================== -->
    <section class="breadcrumb-wrapper">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>Maintenance</h2>
        <ol class="breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/index.html">Home</a></li>
          <li><a href="/support.html">Support</a></li>
          <li class="active">Maintenance</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <!-- ==========================
    	BREADCRUMB - END
    =========================== -->

    <div id="content" class="container default">

      <div class="row">
        <div style="margin-bottom: 50px;" class="col-xs-12 show">
          <h1>Exchange a TIM</h1>
          <p><a class="btn btn-primary"
              href="mailto:support@roos.com?subject=Request%20RMA?body=System:%20%3cSerial%3e,%20TIM:%20%3cSerial%3e,%20Full%20Diags%20attached."><i
                class="fa-solid fa-envelope"></i> Request RMA</a>&nbsp; Send Diagnostic Data with Show
            Full Results Option along with System &amp; TIMs Serial Numbers to Support.</p>
          <p>
            Swapping a Failed TIM takes just minutes. All configuration and calibration data is automatically loaded via
            <abbr title="Guru provides enterprise class network storage and workflow.">Guru&trade;</abbr>. Procedure to
            exchange Cassini Test
            Instrument Module (TIM) in 4 steps. Detailed step-by-step process available in the <a href="/refGuide"
              title="Cassini Reference Guide">Cassini Reference Guide</a>,
            Chapter 4: Service, Section 1: Exchanging a New or Repaired TIM.
          </p>
          <h3>Step 1) Prepare for TIM Exchange</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/docs/RBEH-AEGP8B">Import Cal Data </a> into Guru from the email attachment sent to the site's
              primary maintenance manager or contact who opened the RMA (Filename = CalData_&lt;serial#&gt;.gzp)</li>
            <li>Unlatch and Lower Testhead from Handler and Remove Fixture.</li>
            <li>Choose <b>System &gt; Check</b> from Main System Window (Fixture removed from Configuration)</li>
            <li>Remove TIM From Shipping Box. Do Not Trash TIM Box, Keep For Return Shipment.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Step 2) Exchange TIM</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Depending on the TIM model number, choose the appropriate step:
              <ul>
                <li>For RI7725 AUX Rack 20 GHz Source, See <a href="https://roos.com/docs/RBEH-8SW447">Exchanging AUX
                    Rack Source TIM</a>.</li>
                <li>For RI8574 ArcaOS EPC TIM or RI8608 Linux NUC, See <a href="https://roos.com/docs/RBEH-APL2LM">Exchange ArcaOS TIM Checklist</a> or <a href="https://roos.com/docs/RBEH-DG2R6U">Exchange Linux NUC Checklist</a>.</li>
                <li>For all others, Pull Top Latch Then Pull Bottom Handle to Release
                  from Testhead.</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Perform <b>System &gt; Check</b> (Instrument(s) Removed from Configuration)</li>
            <li>Install Replacement TIM, one at a time, in the same T-location. <button
                style="background-color: rgb(255,255,255); border: 1px solid; border-radius: 5px;"
                disabled>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;T-4&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</button> <br> Slide TIM Horizontally
              Until Touching Testhead, Slide TIM Vertically Until In Position, Press Top Latch Down</li>
            <li>Perform <b>System &gt; Check </b>(Instrument Added to Configuration, repeat previous step)</li>
                          <ul><li>For RI8545 20 GHz TestSet (Testhead instrument), See <a href="https://roos.com/docs/RBEH-ASAUGU">Cal
                    Data Activation (Testhead)</a>.</li></ul>
            <li>Start Warm Up Timer (20 minutes). Choose <b>Tester &gt; Save</b> menu from the Configuration window to save the instrument names.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Step 3) Verify and Release to Production</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Latch Diag/Cal Plate from Diagnostic Kit</li>
            <li>Perform <b>System &gt; Check </b>(Fixture Added to Configuration)</li>
            <li>Open Configuration window ( <b>System &gt; Tester</b> ) Perform Targeted Diags (<b>Tester &gt;
                Diagnose</b>, Select Plans for Instrument Model #s)
              <div class="alert alert-warning" style="margin: 15px 0;"><b>NOTICE!</b> Wait for the TIM to warm up at
                least 20 minutes before running Diagnostics</div>
            </li>
            <li>If Diags Pass, Unlatch Diag/Cal Plate, Latch Fixture and Release to Production (Skip To Step 4)</li>
            <li>To Confirm Recent Cal Data, Select Instrument from Config Window, RMB Menu Choose <b>Calibration &gt;
                Restore... &gt; OK</b>. If One or More Dates Do Not Appear, Import Cal Data (contact Manager) and Choose
              <b>System &gt; Startup</b>. Perform
              Targeted Diags Again.
            </li>
            <li>If Diags Fail, Run Full Diags, then Perform Targeted Calibration (TIM or Complete RF System)</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Step 4) Document and Return Failed TIM</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Prepare return shipping documents according to Return Shipment Instructions (RSI) (HTC, value,
              description)</li>
            <li>Write Serial Number if not already included on RSI. Include any relevant information (Diag data,
              symptoms)</li>
            <li>Ship Failed TIM in provided TIM Box. If a TIM Box is not available, follow these Safe Packing
              Instructions.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <div style="margin-bottom: 50px;" class="col-xs-12">
          <h2>When to Calibrate?</h2>
          <p>
            Always import the provided factory calibration data (.gzp). Never calibrate "stand-alone" TIMs outside of
            the standard calibration interval because they only interface with the Fixture and DIB and so their
            Calibration data is valid when used in any tester. Calibration may only be needed when changing TIMs with
            "RF System" connections. The "RF System" is any cable connection between TIMs found on the Diag/Cal
            Interface Plate (Diag Fixture). Fixture Calibration may be required on Fixtures that use the
            "calPerTester=True" attribute in the Device Connection Editor. Always follow the <a href="/refGuide"
              title="Cassini Reference Guide">Cassini Reference Guide</a>, CH 5: Diagnose procedure after changing TIMs
            and prior to releasing the tester for production.
          </p>
          <table id="standaloneTIMs" class="table table-striped table-bordered" style="width: 100%;">
            <caption style="font-weight: bold;">"Stand-Alone" TIM Models</caption>
            <thead style="width: 100%">
              <tr>
                <th>TIM Model</th>
                <th>Description of Stand-alone TIMs (Standard Calibration Interval)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8535</td>
                <td>Universal Digital TIM (1 Yr)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8546</td>
                <td>Device Power TIM (1 Yr)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8572</td>
                <td>Waveform TIM (1 Yr)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8575</td>
                <td>Phase Noise Measure (Lifetime) - Return to factory</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8589</td>
                <td>FET Pulser TIM (1 Yr)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8594</td>
                <td>Waveform TIM with Low Noise Clock (1 Yr)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>RI8612</td>
                <td>Device Power Plus (1 Yr)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
